computer and what I see is  remarkable not only is his a ordered  acepted which means that the wall has  split into two but he's actually leaking  blood from it this man needs urgent  surgery but he needed it hours ago while  I was sleeping I ran over to him to try  and undo my mistake in fact I want to  take a scalpel out there and there and  get going but I can't his name's Jack  he's got a shiny bald head flanked by  unruly gray curls he's got this  fantastic moustache sitting on top of a  very broad toothless smile and somehow  he's managing to eat peanut M&amp;Ms which  are perched on his potbelly and he's  meant to be fasting but I can't blame  him because they're my favorite too any  and he offers me one we shake hands and  we look each other in the eye and and he  says to me get a David boy am I glad to  see you Eric told me that you were the  man and uh boy boy I'm feeling sheepish  and I look him back in the eye and I say  hardly the man jack in fact I think I  made a bit of an error but those were  the last words that Jack heard because  and it was the funniest sensation ever  but his hand went limp in mine and he  tried to say something but couldn't and  he just let off a little bit of a groan  and behind me on the screen you see his  blood pressure go from 70 to 30 to zero  and people run from everywhere and pound  on his chest but I know it's futile  because his a order is ruptured and  there's no getting him back from that  and it's all because of my era the could  haves and the should'ves of this scene  still keep me up late at night but it  was only because of Jack that this year  a colleague of mine at Stanford and I  built a deep learning algorithm that  could automatically diagnose this exact  emergency and recommend a treatment plan  Jack would have been flown to hospital I  would have been woken up but he would  have had his life saved and this is just  one example of why when people say AI in  health care it's just way more than a  buzz term to me but while I'm up late at  night thinking about Jack I also can't  help but wonder what does this mean for  the future of medicine would it really  be safest if we just distill all of our  patients you guys down to processes of  pattern recognition and image detection  and if so when you or your loved ones  are at your sickest and most vulnerable  who do you want by your side  man or machine well only a few years  before Jack I was sitting on the other  side of the fence in the waiting room  with the person I loved most  my sister Jackie waiting for a diagnosis  and waiting for a treatment plan and I  do wonder how that day might have gone  were we to meet a machine say an AI  oncologist because any good AI  oncologist would need data inputs and  these were Jackie's age 16 symptoms  intermittent pelvic pain loss of weight  and bloating findings at operation a  large malignant mass now if medicine is  just about making the right diagnosis  and giving the right treatment this  would be a five minute consultation and  I could write that code today your data  outputs would be diagnosis a rare  malignant tumor treatment plan twelve  months of chemotherapy and radiotherapy  prognosis uncertain but surely even a  half-assed AI oncologist would want to  know if we had any questions because  that day Jackie had plenty a eye  oncologist am I going to lose my hair  and how am I going to look well you will  easy here you'll look bald a eye  oncologist I love children I'm 16 now am  I going to be able to have children  after all this chemotherapy and  radiotherapy the statistics would  suggest unlikely but I'm I'm not an AI  gynecologist  ái oncologist am i going to die and as  the tears streamed down our faces surely  any half-decent AI oncologist would be  able to use image detection to see those  tears and robotic ly dispense us with a  tissue but is this really the kind of  care that we want because at that moment  what these two petrified kids needed was  so much more than a diagnosis and  treatment plan in fact that was the easy  bit we didn't need some watson or  advanced clinical decision support  software what we needed and what we got  thankfully was a very human person who  would stand up walk over to the other  side of the desk put his arms around us  and say that despite what the algorithm  said everything was going to be okay  that he and not some fandangled machine  would be with us every step of the way  yes  ai is going to do amazing things and  it's going to help all of us in this  room it's going to save lives it's going  to slash medical error and it's going to  improve access to care but being a great  doctor is about so much more than making  the right diagnosis it requires a  delicacy a touch and a judgement that  even the best technologists will never  be able to replicate so as we build  amazing machines to make you guys safer  it will not be the computer scientists  who become the great doctors of the  future it will be humans like Jackie who  overcame the algorithms and just  graduated medical school last year  who  the humans like Jackie who possess the  skills that are just way too difficult  to code for  [Applause]  [Music]

